bra with the example of group cohomology. The book culminates with the description\nof the abelian extensions of local number ﬁelds, as well as the celebrated Kronecker-Weber\ntheorem, in both the local and global cases. The material will ﬁnd use across disciplines,\nincluding number theory, representation theory, algebraic geometry, and algebraic topology.\nWritten for beginning graduate students and advanced undergraduates, this book can be used\nin the classroom or for independent study.\npierre guillot is a lecturer at the University of Strasbourg and a researcher at the Institut de\nRecherche Mathématique Avancée (IRMA). He has authored numerous research papers in the\nareas of algebraic geometry, algebraic topology, quantum algebra, knot theory, combinatorics,\nthe theory of Grothendieck’s dessins d’enfants, and Galois cohomology.\nwww.cambridge.org\n© in this web service Cambridge University Press & Assessment--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContents - A Gentle Course in Local Class Field Theory (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/gentle-course-in-local-class-field-theory/contents/A096A4B5C705F8F800D291DA6A3ADF60)\nciteturn375search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago;   * Book: A Gentle Course in Local Class Field Theory\n\n# Contents\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  26 October 2018\n\nPierre Guillot\n\nShow author details\n\n* * *\n\nPierre Guillot\n    Affiliation:\n\nUniversité de Strasbourg\n\n* * *\n\nBook contents\n\n  * Frontmatter\n  * Dedication\n  * Epigraph\n  * Contents\n  * Preface\n  * Part I Preliminaries\n  * Part II Brauer Groups\n  * Part III Galois Cohomology\n  * Part IV Class Field Theory\n  * Appendix: background material\n  * Notes and further reading\n  * References\n  * Index\n\nGet access\n\n* * *\n\n## Summary\n\nA summary is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.\n\nImage: Image of the first page of this content. For PDF version, please use the ‘Save PDF’ preceeding this image.'\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Chapter\n\nInformation\n    \n\nA Gentle Course in Local Class Field Theory\n\nLocal Number Fields, Brauer Groups, Galois Cohomology\n\n, pp. vii - x\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108377751 [Opens in a new window]\n\nPublisher: Cambridge University Press\n\nPrint publication year: 2018\n\n* * *\n\n## Accessibility standard: Unknown\n\n  * Contents\n  * Pierre Guillot, Université de Strasbourg\n  * Book: A Gentle Course in Local Class Field Theory\n  * Online publication: 26 October 2018\n\nYour Kindle email address\n\n[Input] Please provide your Kindle email.\n\n[Input]@free.kindle.com [Input]@kindle.com (service fees apply)\n\nAvailable formats [Input] PDF Please select a format to save.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrimes (https://dacox.people.amherst.edu/primes.html)\nciteturn375search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The third edition of Primes of the Form x^{2} + ny^{2} was published in 2022 by the American Mathematical Society. ...   * Solutions to all exercises, written by Roger Lipsett and David Cox, are included.\n## Image: cover of book Primes of the Form x^{2} + ny^{2}:\nFermat, Class Field Theory, and Complex Multiplication\n\n## by David A. Cox\nAmherst College\nwith contributions by Roger Lipsett\n\n## American Mathematical Society\n\n## Original Edition, 1989\nPaperback Edition, 1997\nSecond Edition, 2013\nThird Edition with Solutions, 2022\n\n## The Third Edition\n\nThe third edition of Primes of the Form x^{2} + ny^{2} was published in 2022 by the American Mathematical Society. The main features of the third edition are:\n\n  * Solutions to all exercises, written by Roger Lipsett and David Cox, are included.\n  * Section 15 on Shimura Reciprocity has been rewritten for clarity.\n  * There is now a unified Reference section.\n  * All exercises have been checked for clarity and correectness.\n  * A few new exercises and references have been added.\n  * All known errata in the second edition have been corrected.\n\n## Errata in the Third Edtion\n\nA list of known errata in the third edition is available in pdf\n\n## The Second Edition\n\nThe second edition of Primes of the Form x^{2} + ny^{2} was published in 2013 by John Wiley & Sons. The main features of the second edition are:\n\n  * There is a new Section 15 on Shimura reciprocity, based on the work of Peter Stevenhagen and Alice Gee.\n  * The new Additional References section contains references not cited in the first edition. Some of these new references are mentioned in the text; the others are intended for further reading.\n  * The new Exercise 12.31 at the end Section 12 shows how Ramanujan could have done one of Weber's singular moduli computations.\n  * All of the known errata in the first edition have been corrected.\nThe other changes in the text are minor, mostly to enhance clarity, improve formatting, and simplify some of the proofs.\n\n## Errata in the Second Edition\n\nThe list of known errata as of June 19, 2022 in the second edition is available in pdf .\n\n## Errata in the First Edition\n\nLists of errata are available for the two printings of the hardback first edition. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCambridge University Press (https://assets.cambridge.org/97805215/92710/frontmatter/9780521592710_frontmatter.pdf)\nciteturn375search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; 978-0-521-59271-0 - Probability Theory: The Logic of Science ... Jaynes\nCambridge University Press\n978-0-521-59271-0 - Probability Theory: The Logic of Science\nE. T. Jaynes\nFrontmatter\nMore information\nPreface\nThe following material is addressed to readers who are already familiar with applied math-\nematics, at the advanced undergraduate level or preferably higher, and with some ﬁeld,\nsuch as physics, chemistry, biology, geology, medicine, economics, sociology, engineering,\noperations research, etc., where inference is needed.1 A previous acquaintance with proba-\nbility and statistics is not necessary; indeed, a certain amount of innocence in this area may\nbe desirable, because there will be less to unlearn.\nWe are concerned with probability theory and all of its conventional mathematics, but\nnow viewed in a wider context than that of the standard textbooks. Every chapter after the\nﬁrst has ‘new’ (i.e. not previously published) results that we think will be found interesting\nand useful. Many of our applications lie outside the scope of conventional probability\ntheory as currently taught. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProbability Theory (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/probability-theory/9CA08E224FF30123304E6D8935CF1A99)\nciteturn375search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; Jaynes dispels the imaginary distinction between 'probability theory' and 'statistical inference', leaving a logical unity and simplicity, which provides greater technical power and flexibility in applications. ... New results are discussed, along with applications of probability theory to a wide variety of problems in physics, mathematics, economics, chemistry and biology.It contains many exercises and problems, and is suitable for use as a textbook on graduate level courses involving data analysis.\n\n×\n\n    * E. T. Jaynes\n\n    * Edited by G. Larry Bretthorst\n\n    * E. T. Jaynes\n\n    * Edited by G. Larry Bretthorst\n\nShow more authors Image\n\n  * You may already have access via personal or institutional login\n\n  *     * Check access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\n  * Select format\n\nDigital Unavailable Hardback Available\n\n  * Publisher:\n    Cambridge University Press\n\nPublication date:\n    05 September 2012\n    10 April 2003\n\nISBN:\n    9780511790423\n    9780521592710\n\nDOI:\n    https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511790423\n\nDimensions:\n    (247 x 174 mm)\n\nWeight & Pages:\n    1.64kg, 753 Pages\n\nDimensions:\n    \n\nWeight & Pages:\n    \n\nSubjects:\n    Physics and Astronomy, Theoretical Physics and Mathematical Physics, Applied Probability and Stochastic Networks, Statistics and Probability\n\nYou may already have access via personal or institutional login\n\n*   * Check access\n\nCheck if you have access via personal or institutional login\n\nLog in Register\n\nSelected: Digital\n\nAdd to cart View cart Buy from Cambridge.org\n\nSubjects:\n    Physics and Astronomy, Theoretical Physics and Mathematical Physics, Applied Probability and Stochastic Networks, Statistics and Probability\n\nInformation\n\nInformation\n\nContents\n\nMetrics\n\nAccessibility\n\n### Book description\n\nThe standard rules of probability can be interpreted as uniquely valid principles in logic. In this book, E. T. Jaynes dispels the imaginary distinction between 'probability theory' and 'statistical inference', leaving a logical unity and simplicity, which provides greater technical power and flexibility in applications. This book goes beyond the conventional mathematics of probability theory, viewing the subject in a wider context. New results are discussed, along with applications of probability theory to a wide variety of problems in physics, mathematics, economics, chemistry and biology. It contains many exercises and problems, and is suitable for use as a textbook on graduate level courses involving data analysis. The material is aimed at readers who are already familiar with applied mathematics at an advanced undergraduate level or higher. The book will be of interest to scientists working in any area where inference from incomplete information is necessary.\n\n### Reviews\n\n‘This is not an ordinary text. It is an unabashed, hard sell of the Bayesian approach to statistics. It is wonderfully down to earth, with hundreds of telling examples. Everyone who is interested in the problems or applications of statistics should have a serious look.’\n\nSource: SIAM News\n\n'This book could be of interest to scientists working in areas where inference of incomplete information should be made.'\n\nSource: Zentralblatt MATH\n\n'… the author thinks for himself … and writes in a lively way about all sorts of things. It is worth dipping into it if only for vivid expressions of opinion. The annotated References and Bibliography are particularly good for this.'\n\nSource: Notices of the American Mathematical Society\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlgebraic Number Theory | London Mathematical Society (https://www.lms.ac.uk/publications/algebraic-number-theory)\nciteturn375search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Cassels and A.Fröhlich ... The book is a standard text for taught courses in algebraic number theory.\nImage: Publications\n\n# Algebraic Number Theory\n\nImage: Book cover of Algebraic Number Theory\n\n## About\n\nAlgebraic Number Theory, edited by J.W.S. Cassels and A. Fröhlich\nPublished by the London Mathematical Society\nISBN-10: ‎0950273422,  ISBN-13: ‎978-0950273426\n\nFirst printed in 1967, this book has been essential reading for aspiring algebraic number theorists for more than fifty years. It contains the lecture notes from an instructional conference held in Brighton in 1965, which was a milestone event that introduced class field theory as a standard tool of mathematics. There are landmark contributions from Serre and Tate. The book is a standard text for taught courses in algebraic number theory. This Second Edition includes a valuable list of errata compiled by mathematicians who have read and used the text over the years.\nOrders may be placed via the major online bookstores (e.g. Amazon UKImage, Amazon USAImage; see the list of distribution partners). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantum Statistical Inference - Physics and Probability (https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/physics-and-probability/quantum-statistical-inference/9AC397D7258124322C7F3299175E1083)\nciteturn375search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; Jaynes has been an eloquent advocate for a compelling hypothesis: Probability Theory as Logic.\n\nIntroduction\n\nJaynes has been an eloquent advocate for a compelling hypothesis: Probability Theory as Logic. That is, probabilities represent degrees of belief; probability theory develops and applies universal principles of logical inference from incomplete information. Two of his primary interests have been Bayesian probability theory and the interpretation of quantum mechanics. Bayesian probability theory yields inferences by systematically and consistently combining new data with prior knowledge. Jaynes pioneered the maximum entropy (ME) class of Bayesian methods for density function estimation (Jaynes, 1983), which has been applied successfully to numerous data analysis problems.\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Chapter\n\nInformation\n    \n\nPhysics and Probability\n\nEssays in Honor of Edwin T. Jaynes\n\n, pp. 223 - 238\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511524448.021 [Opens in a new window]\n\nPublisher: Cambridge University Press\n\nPrint publication year: 1993\n\n  * Quantum Statistical Inference\n  *     * By R. N. Silver, Theoretical Division, MS B262 Los Alamos National Laboratory, Los Alamos, New Mexico 87545\n  * Edited by W. T. Grandy, Jr, University of Wyoming, P. W. Milonni, Los Alamos National Laboratory\n  * Book: Physics and Probability\n  * Online publication: 21 October 2009\n  * Chapter DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511524448.021--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDavid A. Cox (https://dacox.people.amherst.edu/)\nciteturn375searc